frame.asks_ops:
Stephanie (your seat runs on the operations doctrine), please bring me the following.

(1) KPI status in writing, confirmed from the brief, not assumed. Is any quality, FAA, certification or supply-chain problem live? I read none [BX-1271]. The Board will not take anything without your result.

(2) Written assurance on the MAX phase-down for the Board's Aerospace Safety Committee [BG-0043]. Capacity goes 641 (2045), 556 (2047), 428 (2050), 299 (2053), 214 (2055), 0 (2060).
- In 2045-47 we are demand-limited: 130 idle slots in 2045, 74 in 2047. A line running below plan loses people and suppliers.
- From 2050 the phase-out binds: customers want 437 against 428, and 305 against 214 in 2055.
Give me your results on:
- quality KPIs on a shrinking line;
- fragile suppliers funded and hot [B-2330, BX-1243];
- LEAP-1B and materials secured for our own lines;
- trained people kept;
- the in-service fleet supported in full [BX-1273];
- how we treat the last MAX customers when capacity binds. I would rather tell them plainly than run the line unstable [BX-1274].

(3) Two-front strain: confirm the counts. Re-engine alone 0; beside a 10-year fps or via Embraer 2; beside a 7-year 5.

(4) 787 Re-engine readiness, only for the record, since my lean is hold:
- a widebody engine committed and ready by the 2050 EIS (the GEnx upgrade has been ready since 2033; Rolls-Royce has launched no widebody engine);
- the amended type certificate before the line converts;
- no production-certification concurrency [BX-1263];
- Charleston stable [BX-1317].

(5) Engine readiness for a future fps. Codes 5, 6 and 7 give CFM plus the Joint Venture at a 2055 EIS. Is that two real sources or two on paper?
- Joint Venture capacity and durability, given P&W's open GTF fleet work;
- CFM capacity shared with NGSA's ramp [BX-1264, BX-1298].

(6) Solo or via Embraer on IP [BX-1330]. Embraer is $4.04B below the Solo 10-year in every column, and the CFM-Embraer partnership has been ready since 2042. Does it bring anything we lack?

(7) Renton. Is there any operational reason to move fps_site now, when its item value is 0? The full $3.0B saving holds if we choose Renton at least 3 years before EIS.

(8) The 2050 handover question.
- A 10-year launch in 2050 enters service in 2060, the year the MAX reaches zero: a cold handover.
- A 7-year launch in 2050 enters in 2057, while the MAX still builds about 128 a year.
What does each mean for workforce, suppliers and quality? What must we keep warm now, at no cost on the grid, so a 2050 decision is executable either way: workforce, suppliers, engines, the Renton plan, and no concurrency?

(9) The operations side of the franchise view: the narrowbody tail plan, and the widebody and services base that carries the company beyond it.

Board risk to note: the Board says it will veto any launch that lacks your written KPI, supplier and strain results, and any Re-engine without a 2050 engine and the type certificate before conversion.

tests.numbers:
Yes: 2045 is the last warm handover. A 2045 launch enters service with the MAX at 342 a year (7-year, EIS 2052) or 214 (10-year, EIS 2055), which is 50% or 31% of the 2043 rate of 684. A 2050 7-year launch (EIS 2057) meets the MAX at 128 a year (10.7 a month), with MAX volume down 81%. A 2050 10-year launch (EIS 2060) meets it at zero, after 43 in 2059. That is a cold restart of narrowbody people and suppliers, and by 2050 Boeing will have gone 23 years without a major development. The grid does not price this; the doctrine slip prior (+3 years, range +2 to +5) is the stress to apply.
Does it change the order? No. Launching now is -3.31 against Do Nothing. The Board's trend puts a 2050 launch at -2.0 to -2.6, which is $0.71-1.31B better, and holding keeps the choice not to launch at all. For the cold handover to reverse that, its unpriced cost would have to reach about $13-39B in 2055-60 money (PV factors of 18-30 at 10.5%; my inference, Malave to check). A launch now also still fails the S3 floor.
Keep warm at no cost:
(1) a core design team on fps maturation and production-system work;
(2) fragile suppliers funded through the tail, with tooling and qualifications kept;
(3) capacity and durability talks with CFM and the Joint Venture;
(4) the Renton plan, set against the glide path;
(5) a development plan with the type certificate before rate production, and no fixed price [BX-1263, BX-1262].
On the 2050 grid, the 7-year's warmer handover should count operationally only if the two ramps are within epsilon

