board_guidance.expect_to_see:
For each Board item, and for the package as a whole, the Board expects the following.

(1) The grid row of the submitted orders, with the default row beside it, column by column for S1-S7.
- Default package (NGSA hold, A320 Alt plan, A350 hold, Delay Tactics none): +11.62 / +11.54 / +11.55 / +10.83 / +11.54 / +11.52 / +11.43.
- With an A350 Re-engine: +12.51 / +12.43 / +12.44 / +11.72 / +12.43 / +11.01 / +10.93.
- The Re-engine's item value is therefore +0.89 in S1-S5 and -0.51 / -0.50 in S6-S7.
- For any Delay Tactics move, the same row without it.

(2) The CEO's expected scenario and weights, revisited on Boeing's 2045 line:
- no fps date, and 'the business case isn't there yet';
- the 787 and 777X lines 'stable', with effort on 'the airplanes our customers fly today';
- the MAX phasing out from 2044.
The package should name the worst plausible column for each row: S4 (+10.83) for the default package, S7 (+10.93) with a Re-engine. It should also name the worst column for the Re-engine item itself: S6 (-0.51). If the expected scenario is S6 or S7, the Re-engine loses in it and fails the Board's downside test.

(3) The CFO's peak-cash arithmetic for 2045-2049:
- the Re-engine at $5B / 5 = $1.0B a year;
- no two-front strain, since NGSA development ended in 2036;
- any Alt-plan tail and any extra-line spend in those years;
- all against the $5.23B line (EUR 4,753m at $1.10/EUR).
Also: net cash after the NGSA cash years, the payout proposal, any buyback authority and confirmation that no equity raise is needed. If he applies his robustness test to the Re-engine (negative in S6-S7), the Board wants his finding and whether it binds.

(4) Mr Wagner's results:
- safety and quality on NGSA in service;
- the ramp to about rate 100 and the extra lines from 2045, each step gated on quality KPIs;
- the last A320s, and LEAP-1A and GTF tail contracts that cover 2045-2046 without drawing on fleet spares;
- supply crunch;
- for a Re-engine: engineering capacity alongside the NGSA ramp, staffing from recruitment, and the engine and its maturity for entry into service in 2050.

(5) The ExCo veto checks, and any override with its margin. None is expected. An override of a soft veto on the Re-engine cannot pass: its margin over hold is +0.89, below $1B in every column. The game's one override stays in reserve.

(6) The public statement, a capital-markets announcement reserved to the Board. It should report NGSA as in service, because the round-2 line 'remains in development ... second half of the 2030s' is now out of date. It should give windows, not dates, for any Re-engine, and carry no word of Delay Tactics and no state money.

(7) If the A350 is held: the forgone value on the record (+0.89 in S1-S5) and the ExCo's view of later windows.

(8) A board_response to each of this round's recommendations. No round-2 review recommendation is outstanding.

recommendations.text:
If the Re-engine goes ahead:
- announce it in windows (entry into service around the turn of the decade), not by date;
- Rolls-Royce is the company's default A350 engine maker, and Mr Wagner should confirm the engine's maturity for 2050;
- Re-engine engineering staffing should come from recruitment and internal development, not from the NGSA ramp.
The Board will not cancel the Re-engine in reaction to a Boeing 787 Re-engine unless Boeing's enters service first and the remaining spend loses at least $1B. A same-year 2050 entry into service would not qualify.

Safety oversight of the ramp. At the Board's twice-yearly safety review, Mr Wagner should report on:
- NGSA in-service safety and quality;
- each NGSA rate step to about 100 a month and each extra-line step from 2045 (5 a month a year, up to 20), each gated on conformance, concessions and zero safety-related escapes;
- the last A320 deliveries, with the LEAP-1A and GTF tail contracts covering them without drawing on fleet spares.
The 60/40 shortfall was set in 2040 (47.4%), and nothing on this board changes it. 2045 reads 69.0% and 2050 74.0%. No rate step goes ahead of its quality KPIs to improve the objective.

red_lines:
BOARD WOULD-VETO LIST, carried in full:
- any Delay Tactics move;
- rea350 launch if my expected scenario is S6 or S7, if Wagner fails it on safety, quality, the ramp shield or supply crunch, if it is carried by an override, or if it needs an equity raise or a dividend cut;
- any item that carries an objection neither respected nor properly overridden, or a red-line flag neither struck nor rebutted;
- any change of a320_plan, which is not on offer.

