frame.asks_cfo:
Neil, there is nothing to fund this round, so this is a record round. The Board will read it in 2050, and I trust your cash numbers over my operating dates. Please bring the following.

(1) One governing set of column weights for S1 and S2, summing to 1. My provisional read, for you to test rather than adopt, is S1 0.60 and S2 0.40.
- For S1: Boeing's last word was "the business case isn't there yet", with no date, and I never get ahead of an airframer [PX-0062]. That is also my incrementalism bias, declared.
- For S2: the MAX phases out from 2044 under the public rules, Boeing's delivery share has fallen to 30.4%, and Boeing has named code 7.
Confirm the expected PV (about -2.164 on my weights; -2.160 to -2.165 for any p(S2) from 0 to 0.5), the worst column (S2, -2.17) and the booked number (-2.16).

(2) Confirm that 2045 adds no R&D, that the 2035 fold wrote off $0, and that total R&D on the brief is $2.00B, equal to P&W's Joint Venture share. That means the round-2 low end (a second $2.00B share) did not happen. This closes the 2045 standing-commitment review the Board asked for.

(3) The drift, in plain numbers: from -1.53 (2035 baseline) and +0.68 (round-2 S4 forecast, not booked) to -2.16. On my rounded read of the brief's table:
- 2037-38: about +0.10 PV;
- 2039-40: about -0.61 PV;
- 2041-56: about +0.41 PV;
- net about -0.10, against the brief's engine value of -0.16.
Decompose the $0.63B and the $2.84B into:
- (a) the A320-to-NGSA step-down against a status quo that keeps the A320 at 85 a month;
- (b) CFM's ducted engine (+10 pp, half from us once Rolls-Royce had an NB engine) and its Embraer partnership (+5 pp), launched in the same round and absent from the S4 column;
- (c) the Joint Venture's equal-share rule (P&W and Rolls-Royce each at 24.1% in 2044), and whether it took NGSA share from us that our A320neo volume would otherwise have earned;
- (d) supply and demand.
Tell me which of these was foreseeable in 2035 and which was not. I want to own the miss honestly.

(4) S2 and the fps: confirm that a code-7 fps entering service in 2055 is worth -0.01 to us on the board's horizon (NB counted to 2056), so nothing should be spent or conceded to win it. Record, without booking, any value past 2056 and any exposure if Boeing chose a code without us.

(5) The envelope check:
- $0 used of the roughly $6B a year;
- the dividend covered;
- debt on its path back to pre-ASR levels, with buybacks as the fill-in once debt is there;
- the one-time stretch unused and not needed.
If Eddy says capacity for the ramp to 925 engines a year (2049) needs capital, show it against Do Nothing and against a buyback.

(6) Ground 3 on the formed Joint Venture terms going to the Finance Committee:
- gear and GTF Advantage core P&W-owned, with no licence or transfer;
- no balancing payment;
- P&W's share not below half;
- P&W modules supplied at standard terms;
- the GTF risk-sharing partners behind our half, with no partner money booked until signed.
Also tell me how the Joint Venture programme NPV should be reviewed with the Board each year.

(7) Your four-ground veto check on hold/hold, on the other moves and on my draft statement. On ground 4, tell me whether "responsible bets with responsible paybacks" reads as a claim of payback when Capitalise is not met. If it does, I drop it.

(8) Looking to 2050: is there any order a 2050 brief could offer that changes our position? Record it; do not book it.

